= The KAPD

On 4 and 5 April, in a Berlin restaurant, the opposition we expelled at Heidelberg founds the **Communist Workers' Party of Germany**. It claims thirty-eight thousand members, strongest in Berlin, Hamburg and Bremen. It rejects parliament and the unions, and says the Kapp days proved it right: [? if kapp_statement = 1 : even our own Zentrale would not defend the Republic. ?][? if kapp_statement != 1 : our Zentrale ran after the social democrats. ?]

Two of its members, Jan Appel and Franz Jung, are already on their way to Moscow to apply for membership of the International. They seized a fishing steamer off Cuxhaven at gunpoint to get there.

- @members: Write to their members in the works.
- @joint: Offer joint action in the strikes.
- @moscow: Ask Moscow to keep them out of the International.

@members
subtitle: A few hundred will come back each week.
on-arrival: {!
Q.kapd_members -= 4;
RG.district('berlin', {members: 0.06});
Q.lean_luxemburgist += 1;
!}

Our leaflets say: you left over the elections, and the strike against Kapp was won by unity.

@joint
subtitle: They are revolutionaries. Our moderates will say we are rewarding a split.
on-arrival: kapd_relation += 15; lean_offensive += 1

In Berlin and on the Hamburg docks our cells and theirs agree to stand together in strikes.

@moscow
subtitle: Moscow listens to us, and will remember the favour.
on-arrival: {!
Q.kapd_relation -= 10;
RG.ecci('zinoviev', 3);
!}

Zinoviev replies that the Executive will hear both sides.
